From c826d22d1881b49164aa9ded0096dbe2293be771 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 01:53:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] linker: lccc-ld userspace mode, hot-path fixes (-36% link
 time), real benchmark

Three coordinated pieces of the LINKER_FOLLOWUP plan:

1. lccc-ld GAINS THE STANDARD USERSPACE MODE. Previously it hard-errored
   without -T/-r ('requires a linker script'), so 'make LD=lccc-ld' and
   direct gcc-style invocations (crt1.o ... -lc --dynamic-linker ...)
   were impossible - and the user's own 'lccc-ld -v' CLI expectation
   only half-true. It now routes script-less links through the SAME
   link_builtin/link_shared pipeline the compiler driver uses (archive
   group semantics, PLT/GOT, RELRO, eh_frame_hdr, gc-sections, --wrap,
   -u, --defsym, -z keywords, -static, -shared, rpath/soname/
   version-script forwarding). --whole-archive without -T fails loudly
   instead of silently dropping members; non-standard --dynamic-linker
   values warn instead of diverging silently.

2. HOT-PATH FIXES, measured with the new permanent LCCC_LD_TIME phase
   profiler (link.rs phases + emit_exec zones):
   - register_symbols_elf64: globals.reserve(obj.symbols.len()) - the
     doubling cascade re-hashed every existing key on growth
     (40k-symbol object: 23.1 -> 12.9 ms);
   - emit_exec: the 'snapshot' globals.clone() deep-copied the whole
     map (40k String keys) although globals is never touched afterwards
     - replaced by a reborrow (relocs zone 9.0 -> 3.5 ms);
   - symtab sort: cached big-endian u64 name-prefix key decides almost
     every comparison in one instruction instead of memcmp over long
     common prefixes ('F1','F12',...), full byte compare only on prefix
     ties - identical order (5.3 -> 2.7 ms); entries/strtab pre-sized;
     single-lookup symbol registration via get_mut (no key re-clone).
   big-text profile end-to-end: 77 -> 49 ms (-36%).

3. tests/linker/bench_linker.py REWRITTEN as a real differential
   benchmark: 4 synthetic profiles (many-objects/big-text/many-syms/
   archive) + sqlite3+shell real link, every output EXECUTED and its
   stdout verified (fast-but-wrong scores zero), best-of-3, vs bfd and
   mold. Current standings (2-core VM):
     many-objects 19ms vs bfd 77 / mold 95   (fastest)
     archive      23ms vs bfd 51 / mold 15
     big-text     51ms vs bfd 101 / mold 30
     many-syms    43ms vs bfd 94 / mold 27
     sqlite-exe   16ms vs mold 16 (bfd fails on this invocation)
   mold's remaining big-text/many-syms lead is parallel reloc
   application + faster symtab emission - tracked in LINKER_FOLLOWUP.

PROOF: 86/86 linker differential tests (bfd+mold oracles), 673 lib,
166/166 x86 regression; lccc-ld-linked sqlite3 answers SQL correctly
(sum check); all five bench profiles verified-correct.
